{"runs": "The term 'runs' in this context refers to multiple trials or iterations of an experiment or process, where each trial is conducted with a consistent seed to ensure comparability of results."},
{"runs__have__mean_and_standard_deviation": "The predicate 'have' indicates that the subject 'runs' possess or are associated with certain statistical measures. In this case, it signifies that the runs are characterized by specific values of mean and standard deviation."},
{"mean_and_standard_deviation": "The 'mean and standard deviation' are statistical measures that describe the central tendency and variability of the results obtained from the runs. The mean represents the average outcome, while the standard deviation indicates the degree of variation or dispersion from the average."},
{"results__can_be__directly_compared": "The phrase \"can be\" indicates a possibility or capability, suggesting that the results have the potential to be subjected to a certain action or process, in this case, direct comparison."},
{"directly_compared": "Directly compared refers to the process of evaluating or analyzing the results side by side to identify differences, similarities, or trends, which is crucial for understanding the impact of variables like task difficulty and appearance order on the outcomes."},
{"results__can_be_different_due_to__differences_in_task_difficulty_and_their_appearance_order": "The phrase \"can be different due to\" indicates a causal relationship where variations in the results are attributed to specific factors. In this context, it highlights how the outcomes of the trials can vary based on certain conditions or variables."},
{"differences_in_task_difficulty_and_their_appearance_order": "Differences in task difficulty and their appearance order refer to the varying levels of complexity associated with different tasks and the sequence in which these tasks are presented. These factors can significantly influence the results of the trials, leading to variations in performance metrics."},
{"we__include__additional_implementation_details_and_results_in_the_supplementary_material_sm": "The predicate \"include\" indicates that the authors have incorporated or added certain elements to their work. In this context, it signifies that the authors have provided extra information as part of their research output."},
{"additional_implementation_details_and_results_in_the_supplementary_material_sm": "The object \"additional implementation details and results in the Supplementary Material (SM)\" refers to extra information that the authors have provided to supplement their main text. This supplementary material likely contains detailed explanations of how the experiments were conducted, as well as additional data or results that support the findings presented in the main document."},
{"evaluation_metrics": "Evaluation Metrics refer to the criteria used to assess the performance of methods in a given context, particularly in tasks involving learning and memory retention across multiple classes or tasks."},
{"evaluation_metrics__evaluate_methods_using__1_average_final_accuracy_an_or_the_final_accuracy_with_respect_to_all_past_classes_averaged_over_n_tasks_and_2_average_forgetting_fn_or_the_drop_in_task_performance_averaged_over_n_tasks": "The predicate 'evaluate methods using' indicates the process of assessing or judging the effectiveness or quality of methods based on specific criteria or metrics."},
{"1_average_final_accuracy_an_or_the_final_accuracy_with_respect_to_all_past_classes_averaged_over_n_tasks_and_2_average_forgetting_fn_or_the_drop_in_task_performance_averaged_over_n_tasks": "The objects (1) average final accuracy AN, and (2) average forgetting FN, are specific metrics used to evaluate methods. Average final accuracy AN measures the final accuracy with respect to all past classes averaged over N tasks, while average forgetting FN measures the drop in task performance averaged over N tasks."},
