{"reader__is_referred_to__our_sm_for_additional_details_about_these_metrics": "The phrase \"is referred to\" indicates a directive or suggestion for the reader to consult another source for more detailed information. In this context, it serves as a bridge directing the reader to seek further clarification or elaboration on the metrics discussed."},
{"our_sm_for_additional_details_about_these_metrics": "The Supplementary Material (SM) is an additional document or section provided alongside the main text. It contains further implementation details and results, offering a deeper dive into the metrics and their calculation, which are briefly mentioned in the main text."},
{"an": "AN refers to the average final accuracy, which is a key metric used to evaluate the performance of methods in the context of continual learning tasks. It represents the final accuracy with respect to all past classes, averaged over a specified number of tasks.|Here is another description: _AN_ is a metric or method used in the context of evaluating performance across multiple tasks in a machine learning experiment. It is specifically applied in the scenario described in Table 1, which involves different numbers of tasks with varying class distributions.|Here is another description: _AN_ is a metric used in the evaluation of machine learning models, specifically in the context of continual learning tasks on datasets like CIFAR-100 and DomainNet. It is part of a comparative analysis where multiple trials are conducted to assess model performance.|Here is another description: AN refers to a metric or method used in the context of evaluating performance across multiple tasks, specifically in the presence of domain shifts. It is a key component in assessing the robustness and adaptability of models under varying conditions.|Here is another description: _AN_ is a method or metric used in the context of evaluating performance on a series of tasks, specifically within the 10-task ImageNet-R dataset. It is part of an ablation study designed to assess different aspects of model performance."},
{"an__is__the_more_important_metric_and_encompasses_both_method_plasticity_and_forgetting": "The predicate \"is\" in this context signifies that the subject AN is being defined or described as a particular metric. It establishes the identity or nature of AN within the evaluation framework."},
{"the_more_important_metric_and_encompasses_both_method_plasticity_and_forgetting": "The more important metric and encompasses both method plasticity and forgetting refers to the comprehensive nature of the AN metric. It highlights that AN not only measures the final accuracy but also inherently includes aspects of how well a method can adapt to new tasks (plasticity) and how much it forgets previously learned information (forgetting)."},
{"fn": "Average forgetting (FN) is a metric used to evaluate the drop in task performance averaged over N tasks in the context of machine learning evaluations.|Here is another description: _FN_ is a metric used in the context of evaluating performance on multiple tasks within a machine learning experiment, specifically on the ImageNet-R dataset. It is part of a table that summarizes results across different numbers of tasks and classes per task.|Here is another description: _FN_ is a metric used in the context of evaluating performance on datasets like CIFAR-100 and DomainNet. It is specifically designed to measure a particular aspect of model behavior across multiple trials.|Here is another description: FN is a metric used in the context of evaluating machine learning models, specifically in scenarios involving domain shifts. It represents a specific measure related to the performance of models under such conditions.|Here is another description: _FN_ is a metric used in the context of evaluating performance on a series of tasks, specifically in the ablation results on 10-task ImageNet-R. It is a key indicator in assessing the effectiveness of a model or method."},
{"fn__provides__additional_context": "The predicate \"provides\" in this context signifies that the average forgetting metric offers supplementary information or context to the primary evaluation metric, which is the average final accuracy (AN)."},
{"additional_context": "Additional context refers to the extra details or supplementary information that the average forgetting metric (FN) offers beyond the primary metric of average final accuracy (AN). This includes insights into how much performance drops on previously learned tasks when new tasks are introduced."},
{"our_method__evaluated_on__several_well_established_benchmarks": "The phrase \"evaluated on\" indicates the process of assessing the performance, effectiveness, or accuracy of the subject (our method) when applied to a set of standards or tests. In this case, the evaluation is conducted to compare the method's results with those of other established techniques."},
{"several_well_established_benchmarks": "Several well-established benchmarks are standard tests or datasets used in the research community to evaluate the performance of methods or models. These benchmarks provide a common ground for comparison and are recognized for their reliability and relevance in assessing the capabilities of new approaches."},
{"table_1": "Table 1 is a data presentation format used in the context to display the results of various methods evaluated on different benchmarks. It serves as a comparative tool to show the performance metrics of these methods.|Here is another description: Table 1 is a structured data presentation that organizes information into rows and columns, specifically designed to display results in a clear and concise manner."},
{"table_1__provides_results_for__imagenet_r": "The phrase \"provides results for\" indicates a relationship where Table 1 serves as a medium to present or display the outcomes or findings related to a specific dataset or benchmark, in this case, ImageNet-R."},
{"imagenet_r": "ImageNet-R is a benchmark dataset designed to evaluate the performance and robustness of machine learning models, especially in image recognition and classification tasks. It consists of 200 object classes with diverse and challenging image styles, significantly differing from the original ImageNet dataset. This dataset is used to test how well models generalize to new, unseen data and adapt to various visual styles and conditions."},
{"imagenet_r__composed_of__200_object_classes": "The phrase \"composed of\" indicates that ImageNet-R is made up of or includes a specific number of distinct categories or classes. This relation helps to define the structure and scope of the dataset by specifying the number of different object categories it contains."},
{"200_object_classes": "The 200 object classes represent the diverse categories of objects that ImageNet-R is comprised of. These classes include a wide range of image styles such as cartoons, graffiti, and challenging examples from the original ImageNet, which are used to test the robustness and generalizability of machine learning models."},
{"imagenet_r__includes__a_wide_collection_of_image_styles": "The term 'includes' here signifies that ImageNet-R is comprised of or contains a variety of elements within its dataset. This predicate highlights the diversity and comprehensive nature of the dataset in terms of the types of images it encompasses."},
{"a_wide_collection_of_image_styles": "A wide collection of image styles refers to the diverse range of visual representations included in the ImageNet-R dataset. This collection features various artistic and stylistic interpretations of the object classes, such as cartoon, graffiti, and challenging examples from the original ImageNet dataset, aimed at testing the robustness and generalization capabilities of machine learning models."},
{"image_styles": "Image styles refer to the various visual representations and artistic techniques used in images, which can significantly alter the appearance of objects within them."},
{"image_styles__includes__cartoon_graffiti_and_hard_examples_from_the_original_imagenet_dataset": "The term 'includes' denotes the presence or incorporation of specific elements within a broader category or collection. In this context, it signifies that the collection of image styles encompasses certain types of visual representations."},
{"cartoon_graffiti_and_hard_examples_from_the_original_imagenet_dataset": "The object entities\u2014cartoon, graffiti, and hard examples from the original ImageNet dataset\u2014represent distinct visual styles or challenges within the ImageNet-R benchmark. Cartoons are stylized, often simplified representations of objects, graffiti refers to urban art typically found on walls, and hard examples are particularly challenging images from the original ImageNet dataset that test the robustness of image recognition systems."},
{"benchmark": "The benchmark in question is a well-established evaluation method used to assess the performance of various machine learning models. It includes a diverse set of 200 object classes with varied image styles, such as cartoon, graffiti, and challenging examples from the original ImageNet dataset.|Here is another description: The benchmark refers to a standardized test or assessment used to evaluate the performance of different methods or systems. In this context, the benchmark is used to compare the performance of various approaches on a specific task, such as image classification."},
{"benchmark__attractive_because__the_distribution_of_training_data_has_significant_distance_to_the_pre_training_data_imagenet": "The benchmark is considered attractive due to a specific characteristic: the significant distance between the distribution of its training data and the pre-training data from ImageNet. This characteristic creates a fair and challenging problem setting for evaluating models."},
{"the_distribution_of_training_data_has_significant_distance_to_the_pre_training_data_imagenet": "The object of the triple refers to the notable difference in data distribution between the training data used in the benchmark and the pre-training data from ImageNet. This difference is significant and contributes to the benchmark's appeal as it poses a fair and challenging evaluation scenario."},
{"benchmark__provides__a_fair_and_challenging_problem_setting": "The predicate \"provides\" indicates that the benchmark offers or supplies a certain condition or environment for the evaluation of methods. It suggests that the benchmark is designed in a way that it creates a specific setting for the assessment of different techniques."},
{"a_fair_and_challenging_problem_setting": "A fair and challenging problem setting refers to an environment where the evaluation of methods is conducted under conditions that are both equitable and demanding. This means that the benchmark is designed to test the methods in a way that is unbiased and rigorous, ensuring that the results are reliable and meaningful."},
{"original_10_task_benchmark": "The original 10-task benchmark is a well-established evaluation method used to test the performance of various methods and models, particularly in the context of image recognition and classification tasks. It consists of 10 distinct tasks designed to challenge the model's ability to generalize across different object classes and image styles."},
{"original_10_task_benchmark__compared_to__a_smaller_number_of_large_tasks_5_task_and_a_larger_number_of_small_tasks_20_task": "The comparison between the original 10-task benchmark and a configuration with fewer large tasks and more small tasks evaluates how changes in the task distribution affect model performance. This comparison helps in understanding the model's adaptability and efficiency under different task granularities."},
{"a_smaller_number_of_large_tasks_5_task_and_a_larger_number_of_small_tasks_20_task": "A smaller number of large tasks (5-task) and a larger number of small tasks (20-task) represent an alternative benchmark configuration. This setup is designed to test the model's performance when faced with a different distribution of task sizes, potentially offering insights into how the model handles varied task complexities and volumes."},
{"reported_performance_for_dualprompt": "The reported performance for DualPrompt refers to the results obtained by the method in the context of various benchmarks, specifically noting improvements over previously reported figures."},
{"reported_performance_for_dualprompt__is__a_few_percentage_points_higher_compared_to_the_original_dualprompt_paper": "The predicate \"is\" indicates a comparison or a state of being, suggesting that the reported performance for DualPrompt has a certain characteristic or stands in relation to another value."},
{"a_few_percentage_points_higher_compared_to_the_original_dualprompt_paper": "A few percentage points higher compared to the original DualPrompt paper signifies that the newly reported performance metrics for DualPrompt show slight but notable improvements over the results initially published in the original DualPrompt paper."},
{"method__reimplemented_in__a_different_framework_pytorch": "The predicate \"reimplemented in\" indicates that the method was taken from its original form and completely rebuilt or rewritten within a new framework. This process often involves translating the logic and functionality of the method into the conventions and capabilities of the new framework."},
{"a_different_framework_pytorch": "A different framework (PyTorch) refers to PyTorch, a popular open-source machine learning library used for applications such as natural language processing and computer vision. It is known for its dynamic computation graph and ease of use, making it a common choice for researchers and developers in the field of machine learning."},
{"difference_in_performance": "The difference in performance refers to the variation in results observed when comparing the reimplemented method in a different framework (PyTorch) to the original reported performance."},
{"difference_in_performance__suspected_to_be_due_to__averaging_over_different_class_order_shuffling": "The phrase \"suspected to be due to\" indicates a hypothesized cause-and-effect relationship where the difference in performance is thought to result from a specific methodological change."},
{"averaging_over_different_class_order_shuffling": "Averaging over different class-order shuffling describes a methodological approach where the order of classes is shuffled multiple times, and the results are averaged. This technique is suspected to contribute to the observed difference in performance."},
{"implementation_of_l2p_l2p": "The implementation of L2P (L2P++) refers to a reimplementation of the L2P method, enhanced with additional features or improvements, specifically tailored to be comparable with DualPrompt in a fair evaluation setting."},
{"implementation_of_l2p_l2p__performs__significantly_better_than_originally_reported": "The predicate \"performs\" indicates the evaluation of how effectively the subject (L2P++) executes or achieves results in comparison to a baseline or previous reports."},
{"significantly_better_than_originally_reported": "\"Significantly better than originally reported\" describes the outcome where the performance of L2P++ exceeds the performance metrics previously documented for the original L2P method, suggesting improvements or more favorable results under the current evaluation conditions."},
{"l2p__uses__the_same_form_of_prompting_as_dualprompt_for_fair_comparison": "The term \"uses\" in this context indicates that L2P++ incorporates or adopts a specific technique or approach in its implementation. This adoption is strategic, aimed at ensuring a fair comparison with another method, DualPrompt, by standardizing certain aspects of their methodologies."},
{"the_same_form_of_prompting_as_dualprompt_for_fair_comparison": "The same form of prompting as DualPrompt (for fair comparison) refers to a specific prompting strategy employed by DualPrompt that L2P++ also utilizes. This strategy is adopted to create an equitable basis for comparing the performance of L2P++ against DualPrompt, ensuring that any differences in results are due to the core methodologies rather than disparities in prompting techniques."},
{"deep_l2p": "Deep L2P++ is an advanced, enhanced version of the L2P method, designed to improve performance in image recognition benchmarks and specific tasks through the application of deep learning techniques and advanced prompting strategies across multiple layers of neural networks. It is evaluated based on performance metrics and is compared with other methods in various tasks.|Here is another description: Deep L2P++ is an advanced machine learning method designed to improve performance metrics in specific tasks, as indicated by its results in the provided context.|Here is another description: Deep L2P++ is an advanced method or algorithm used in a specific context, likely related to machine learning or data processing, as indicated by its placement among other methods in a comparative table.|Here is another description: Deep L2P++ is a method or algorithm used in a specific context, likely related to machine learning or data processing, as indicated by its placement in a table comparing various methods."},
{"deep_l2p__prompts_at__the_same_5_layers_as_dualprompt_rather_than_only_at_layer_1": "The phrase \"prompts at\" indicates the specific application or implementation of a method within certain layers of a neural network, suggesting a targeted approach to enhance the model's performance by influencing its decision-making process at critical points."},
{"the_same_5_layers_as_dualprompt_rather_than_only_at_layer_1": "The same 5 layers as DualPrompt (rather than only at layer 1) refers to a specific configuration within a neural network where prompts are applied not just at a single layer but across five layers, mirroring the approach used by DualPrompt. This multi-layer prompting is intended to provide a more comprehensive influence on the network's processing, potentially leading to improved outcomes in tasks like image recognition."},
{"deep_l2p__performs__similarly_to_dualprompt": "The term \"performs\" indicates the evaluation of how effectively \"Deep L2P ++\" executes its intended function in comparison to another method. In this context, it refers to the method's ability to achieve similar results on established benchmarks."},
{"similarly_to_dualprompt": "\"Similarly to DualPrompt\" refers to the performance level achieved by DualPrompt, a state-of-the-art method in image recognition tasks. DualPrompt is known for its effective prompting strategy, which \"Deep L2P ++\" aims to match in terms of results on benchmarks like ImageNet-R."},
{"our_method__has__strong_gains_in_average_accuracy_across_all_three_task_lengths": "The predicate \"has\" indicates the possession or achievement of a certain characteristic or result by the subject. In this context, it signifies that the method possesses or achieves strong gains in average accuracy."},
{"strong_gains_in_average_accuracy_across_all_three_task_lengths": "Strong gains in average accuracy across all three task lengths represent a significant improvement in performance metrics over a baseline or comparison method, specifically noting an improvement of up to 4.5% in average accuracy over DualPrompt, across different task durations."},
{"improvement_in_average_accuracy": "The improvement in average accuracy refers to the enhancement in the mean correctness of predictions or results achieved by a method or system when compared to a baseline or another method. In this context, it specifically pertains to the gains observed in the performance of the proposed method across different task lengths."},
{"improvement_in_average_accuracy__is__4_5": "The predicate \"is\" serves as a linking verb that connects the subject, \"improvement in average accuracy,\" to its value or quantification, which is \"4.5%.\" It indicates that the subject has been measured or evaluated to be equivalent to the specified percentage."},
{"coda_p": "CODA-P is a method or model that is frequently evaluated and compared against other techniques in various machine learning tasks. It is noted for its improvements over existing methods and its ability to handle continual distribution shifts, focusing on robustness against semantic and covariate shifts. CODA-P is often present in comparative analyses and performance evaluations alongside other methods."},
{"coda_p__is__our_proposed_method_with_tuned_prompt_length_and_component_set_size": "The predicate 'is' establishes that CODA-P is identified as a specific method, namely the one proposed with certain tuned parameters."},
{"our_proposed_method_with_tuned_prompt_length_and_component_set_size": "Our proposed method with tuned prompt length and component set size refers to CODA-P, which is designed to enhance performance metrics in various task lengths by adjusting specific parameters."},
{"coda_p_s__is__downsized_to_exactly_match_the_number_of_learnable_parameters_as_dualprompt": "The predicate \"is downsized to exactly match the number of learnable parameters as DualPrompt\" indicates a modification or adjustment made to CODA-P-S. This adjustment ensures that CODA-P-S has the same quantity of learnable parameters as DualPrompt, likely for the purpose of a fair comparison or to meet specific model constraints."},
{"downsized_to_exactly_match_the_number_of_learnable_parameters_as_dualprompt": "DualPrompt is a method or model with a certain number of learnable parameters. CODA-P-S is modified to have the same number of learnable parameters as DualPrompt, suggesting that DualPrompt serves as a reference or benchmark in terms of model complexity or capacity."},
{"our_method__has__slightly_higher_average_forgetting_compared_to_dualprompt": "The predicate \"has\" indicates the possession or characteristic of the subject in comparison to another method. In this context, it highlights a specific attribute of our method relative to DualPrompt."},
{"slightly_higher_average_forgetting_compared_to_dualprompt": "Slightly higher average forgetting compared to DualPrompt signifies that while our method shows improvements in average accuracy, it also exhibits a marginal increase in the rate at which previously learned information is forgotten when compared to DualPrompt."},
{"average_accuracy": "Average accuracy refers to the mean value of the correctness of predictions or classifications made by a model over a set of tasks or data points. It is a standard measure used to evaluate the overall performance of machine learning models.|Here is another description: Average accuracy is a measure of the overall performance or effectiveness of a method or model. It represents the mean or average value of accuracy obtained from multiple trials or experiments. In this context, it is being analyzed and compared for different methods or models.|Here is another description: The average accuracy refers to the mean performance metric calculated over multiple trials for a given set of tasks, in this case, the 5 task ImageNet-R dataset."},
{"average_accuracy__is__the_crucial_metric_that_captures_practical_performance": "The phrase \"is\" serves as a linking verb in this context, connecting the subject \"average accuracy\" with its description as \"the crucial metric that captures practical performance.\" It establishes the identity or essential characteristic of the subject."},
{"the_crucial_metric_that_captures_practical_performance": "The crucial metric that captures practical performance indicates the primary measure used to assess how well a model performs in real-world applications. This metric is essential for evaluating the effectiveness and reliability of a model in practical scenarios."},
{"table_1__contains__results_on_imagenet_r": "The predicate 'contains' indicates that Table 1 holds or includes specific data within its structure, providing a comprehensive view of the results it presents."},
{"results_on_imagenet_r": "The results on ImageNet-R refer to the outcomes or performance metrics obtained from experiments or evaluations conducted using the ImageNet-R dataset, which are detailed and summarized in Table 1."},
{"results__are_included_for__5_tasks_40_classes_per_task": "The phrase \"are included for\" indicates that the results are specifically part of the data set presented for certain tasks. It signifies that the results are a component of the overall evaluation for these particular tasks.|Here is another description: The phrase \"are included for\" indicates that the results are part of a set of data or information that has been gathered and presented for specific tasks. It suggests that the results are relevant and have been considered in the evaluation of these tasks."},
{"5_tasks_40_classes_per_task": "The object \"5 tasks (40 classes per task)\" describes a specific configuration within the evaluation framework. It indicates that there are five distinct tasks, each comprising 40 classes, for which results are being reported. This setup allows for a detailed analysis of performance across a varied set of classes.|Here is another description: The object \"5 tasks (40 classes per task)\" describes the specific set of tasks for which the results are reported. These tasks involve classifying images into 40 classes each, and there are five such tasks in total. The tasks are conducted under conditions of domain shifts, where parts of the dataset are randomly removed to simulate different environments or conditions."},
{"results__are_included_for__10_tasks_20_classes_per_task": "The phrase \"are included for\" indicates that the results are part of a set of data that has been compiled or aggregated for specific conditions or parameters. It suggests that the results are relevant and have been considered within the scope of the study or experiment."},
{"10_tasks_20_classes_per_task": "The object \"10 tasks (20 classes per task)\" describes a specific configuration or setup within the experiment. It means that the results are based on a scenario where the experiment was conducted across 10 different tasks, with each task consisting of 20 classes. This setup is one of the conditions under which the results were measured and reported."},
{"results__are_included_for__20_tasks_10_classes_per_task": "The phrase \"are included for\" indicates that the results are part of a comprehensive dataset that encompasses various configurations of tasks. It suggests that the results are specifically aggregated and presented for a certain number of tasks, highlighting their inclusion in the overall analysis."},
{"20_tasks_10_classes_per_task": "The object \"20 tasks (10 classes per task)\" describes a specific configuration within the study where the experiment is divided into 20 distinct tasks, each consisting of 10 classes. This setup allows for a detailed examination of how the model performs under different task granularities and class distributions."},
{"an__gives__the_accuracy_averaged_over_tasks": "The term 'gives' in this context signifies the provision or output of a specific value or metric. It indicates that _AN_ is responsible for calculating or providing a certain measurement related to the performance across tasks.|Here is another description: The predicate \"gives\" indicates that _AN_ provides or yields a specific measurement or result. In this context, it is used to describe how _AN_ produces a certain output related to model performance across multiple tasks."},
{"the_accuracy_averaged_over_tasks": "The accuracy averaged over tasks represents a performance metric that summarizes the effectiveness of a model across multiple tasks. In the context of Table 1, this metric is calculated by _AN_ and is reported as a percentage, reflecting the model's average accuracy when tested on different tasks with varying numbers of classes per task.|Here is another description: The accuracy averaged over tasks is a performance metric that represents the mean accuracy of a model across all tasks in the 10-task ImageNet-R dataset. This metric is crucial for evaluating how well a model generalizes across different tasks."},
{"fn__gives__the_average_forgetting": "The predicate \"gives\" in this context signifies the provision of a specific metric or value. It indicates that _FN_ is responsible for yielding or providing the average forgetting rate, which is a measure used to assess the performance degradation over tasks.|Here is another description: The predicate 'gives' in this context signifies the provision or calculation of a specific metric, _FN_, which is used to quantify a particular aspect of performance in the ablation study."},
{"the_average_forgetting": "The average forgetting, as provided by _FN_, represents a metric that quantifies the degree to which performance declines across different tasks in a machine learning experiment. It is an important indicator of how well a model retains its learning capabilities when faced with new tasks.|Here is another description: The average forgetting, as provided by _FN_, is a measure that indicates the degree to which performance declines over a series of tasks. It is an important metric in evaluating the stability and robustness of a model in continuous learning scenarios."},
{"results__are_reported_over__5_trials": "The phrase \"are reported over\" indicates that the results are being presented or documented across a specified number of trials, providing a measure of consistency and reliability in the findings.|Here is another description: The phrase \"are reported over\" indicates that the results have been collected, analyzed, and presented across a specified number of trials or experiments. It suggests a cumulative or averaged representation of data from multiple instances.|Here is another description: The phrase 'are reported over' indicates that the results are aggregated and presented based on a series of experimental trials, providing a comprehensive view of the performance across multiple iterations."},
