{"we__argue__it_is_very_reasonable_and_reflects_the_strength_of_our_approach": "The predicate \"argue\" indicates that the authors are presenting a reasoned position or viewpoint. They are making a case for why a certain observation or result is valid and beneficial within the context of their research."},
{"it_is_very_reasonable_and_reflects_the_strength_of_our_approach": "The object \"it is very reasonable and reflects the strength of our approach\" is the authors' assertion that the observed slight uptick in forgetting is justifiable and actually demonstrates the robustness and effectiveness of their method in managing and learning new tasks."},
{"our_method__has__a_larger_capacity_to_learn_new_tasks": "The predicate \"has\" indicates possession or a characteristic of the subject. In this context, it signifies that the method possesses a certain quality or capability, specifically related to its performance in learning new tasks."},
{"a_larger_capacity_to_learn_new_tasks": "A larger capacity to learn new tasks refers to the method's enhanced ability or potential to acquire and integrate new information or skills, despite the possibility of forgetting some previously learned tasks. This capacity is considered a strength of the method, allowing it to adapt and improve over time as it encounters more tasks in a sequence."},
{"we__can_sacrifice__marginally_higher_forgetting": "The predicate \"can sacrifice\" indicates the willingness or acceptance by the subject to give up or forgo something in exchange for a perceived greater benefit or advantage. In this context, it suggests that the authors are prepared to accept a certain level of trade-off in performance metrics to achieve other desired outcomes."},
{"marginally_higher_forgetting": "The object \"marginally higher forgetting\" refers to a slight increase in the rate at which previously learned information is forgotten or lost when adopting the method proposed by the authors. Despite this increase, the authors argue that it is a reasonable compromise given the method's other advantages, such as its larger capacity to learn new tasks."},
{"the_forgetting_metric_of_our_method": "The forgetting metric of our method refers to the measure of how much previously learned information is lost as new tasks are acquired. It is an indicator of the method's ability to balance learning new tasks with retaining old knowledge."},
{"the_forgetting_metric_of_our_method__begin_to_converge__to_a_similar_value_as_dualprompt": "Begin to converge indicates that as the task sequence length increases, the forgetting metric of our method starts to approach a consistent value. This suggests that the method's forgetting rate stabilizes over time as it processes more tasks."},
{"to_a_similar_value_as_dualprompt": "To a similar value as DualPrompt means that the forgetting metric of our method is approaching the same level of forgetting observed in DualPrompt. This implies that despite initial differences, both methods exhibit comparable forgetting rates as they handle longer sequences of tasks."},
{"experience_replay__provides__additional_context_of_results": "The term \"provides\" in this context signifies that experience replay offers or supplies a certain benefit or feature to the overall process. Specifically, it indicates that experience replay is instrumental in delivering or contributing to a particular aspect of the results."},
