{"learner__must_support__classification_across_all_classes_seen_up_to_task_n": "The predicate \"must support\" indicates a requirement or necessity for the learner to maintain and utilize its previously acquired knowledge. This involves the capability to classify new inputs based on all classes it has encountered up to the current task."},
{"classification_across_all_classes_seen_up_to_task_n": "The object \"classification across all classes seen up to task _n_\" describes the learner's objective to correctly categorize inputs into any of the classes it has learned from previous tasks, up to the current task _n_. This implies a comprehensive understanding and memory retention of all classes encountered during its learning process."},
{"task_incremental_continual_learning": "Task-incremental continual learning is a method where a model is trained to handle multiple tasks sequentially, with the tasks being clearly defined and labeled throughout the learning process."},
{"task_incremental_continual_learning__is__a_simpler_multi_task_setting": "The term \"is\" here signifies that task-incremental continual learning can be characterized or described as a particular type of learning setting."},
{"a_simpler_multi_task_setting": "A simpler multi-task setting refers to a scenario in machine learning where the complexity is reduced because the tasks are well-defined and labeled, making it easier for the model to distinguish between them during both training and inference."},
{"task_labels": "Task labels refer to the identifiers or markers that specify which task a particular data point or instance belongs to within a multi-task learning framework."},
{"task_labels__are_given_during__both_training_and_inference": "The phrase \"are given during\" indicates that the task labels are provided or made available at specific stages of the learning process."},
{"both_training_and_inference": "Both training and inference represent the two main phases in machine learning: training, where the model learns from data, and inference, where the trained model makes predictions on new, unseen data."},
{"experiments": "The experiments refer to a series of studies or tests designed to evaluate the effectiveness of continual learning methods under specific conditions. These experiments are part of a research effort to understand how learning systems can adapt and improve over time in the face of changing data and tasks."},
{"experiments__contain__both_class_incremental_and_domain_incremental_continual_learning": "The predicate \"contain\" indicates that the experiments include or feature both class-incremental and domain-incremental continual learning. This means that the experiments are designed to test learning systems that must adapt to new classes of data as well as new domains or distributions of data."},
{"both_class_incremental_and_domain_incremental_continual_learning": "Both class-incremental and domain-incremental continual learning represent two types of learning challenges. Class-incremental learning involves adapting to new classes of data over time, while domain-incremental learning involves adapting to new distributions or domains of data. Together, they represent a comprehensive test of a learning system's ability to handle complex, real-world changes in data."},
{"setting": "The setting refers to the experimental setup in continual learning that incorporates both class-incremental and domain-incremental learning simultaneously.|Here is another description: The setting refers to a specific experimental configuration in continual learning that involves both class-incremental and domain-incremental learning simultaneously.|Here is another description: The setting refers to the specific configuration or environment in which an experiment or analysis is conducted. In this context, it denotes the particular setup used for evaluating the performance of different methods.|Here is another description: The setting refers to the specific configuration or environment in which an experiment or analysis is conducted. In this context, it denotes the particular arrangement used for evaluating the performance of different methods."},
