{"we__are_not_worried__by_the_slight_uptick_in_forgetting": "The predicate \"are not worried\" indicates the emotional or cognitive state of the subject regarding a certain situation or outcome. In this case, it shows that the authors are not concerned about a particular aspect of their method's performance."},
{"by_the_slight_uptick_in_forgetting": "The object \"by the slight uptick in forgetting\" refers to a specific observation or result related to the method being discussed. It indicates a minor increase in the rate of forgetting, which is a metric used to evaluate the method's performance in retaining information across tasks."},
{"we__argue__it_is_very_reasonable_and_reflects_the_strength_of_our_approach": "The predicate \"argue\" indicates that the authors are presenting a reasoned position or viewpoint, asserting that the observed increase in forgetting is justifiable and indicative of the robustness of their approach."},
{"it_is_very_reasonable_and_reflects_the_strength_of_our_approach": "The object \"it is very reasonable and reflects the strength of our approach\" encapsulates the authors' assertion that the slight increase in forgetting is not only acceptable but also a testament to the effectiveness and capacity of their method to learn new tasks, despite the trade-off of marginally higher forgetting."},
{"our_method__has__a_larger_capacity_to_learn_new_tasks": "The predicate \"has\" indicates possession or a characteristic of the subject. In this context, it signifies that the method possesses a certain quality or capability, specifically related to its performance in learning new tasks."},
{"a_larger_capacity_to_learn_new_tasks": "A larger capacity to learn new tasks refers to the method's enhanced ability or potential to acquire and integrate knowledge from new tasks it encounters. This capacity is a key feature that distinguishes the method from others, allowing it to perform effectively even as the complexity or number of tasks increases."},
{"we__can_sacrifice__marginally_higher_forgetting": "The predicate \"can sacrifice\" indicates the ability or willingness of the subject to give up or forgo something in order to achieve a greater benefit or to maintain the effectiveness of their approach. It suggests a conscious decision to accept a minor drawback for a significant advantage."},
{"marginally_higher_forgetting": "The object \"marginally higher forgetting\" refers to a slight increase in the rate at which previously learned information is forgotten. This is presented as a trade-off that the subject is willing to accept in order to gain a larger capacity to learn new tasks."},
{"the_forgetting_metric_of_our_method": "The forgetting metric of our method refers to the measurement of how much previously learned information is lost when new tasks are acquired. This metric is crucial in evaluating the performance and efficiency of our approach in continual learning scenarios."},
{"the_forgetting_metric_of_our_method__begin_to_converge__to_a_similar_value_as_dualprompt": "Begin to converge indicates that as the task sequence length increases, the forgetting metrics of our method and DualPrompt start to approach each other. This convergence suggests that the differences in forgetting between the two methods diminish over time."},
{"to_a_similar_value_as_dualprompt": "To a similar value as DualPrompt means that the forgetting metric of our method is becoming comparable to that of DualPrompt. This implies that despite initial differences, both methods exhibit similar levels of forgetting as they handle longer sequences of tasks."},
{"experience_replay__provides__additional_context_of_results": "The term 'provides' in this context indicates that experience replay offers or supplies something beneficial to the process or outcome it is associated with. Here, it suggests that experience replay is a method that contributes to enhancing the learning process by offering something extra."},
{"additional_context_of_results": "The 'additional context of results' refers to the extra information or insights that are gained through the use of experience replay. This could include a better understanding of how the model performs under different conditions, how it adapts to new tasks, or how it compares to other methods like rehearsal."},
