{"coda_prompt__can_scale_prompting_capacity_to__arbitrary_sizes": "The capability of CODA-Prompt to scale its prompting capacity refers to its ability to adjust and expand the size and complexity of the learnable prompt components it uses, allowing it to handle more data or more complex tasks without requiring a complete retraining of the model."},
{"arbitrary_sizes": "Arbitrary sizes in the context of CODA-Prompt refer to the flexibility of the method to scale the number and complexity of the learnable prompt components. This means that the system can be adapted to handle datasets of varying sizes and complexities, making it versatile for different applications and continual learning scenarios."},
{"we__set_a_new_sota_on__both_well_established_benchmarks_and_a_dual_distribution_shift_benchmark": "\"Set a new SOTA on\" means that the authors have achieved a new state-of-the-art performance. This indicates that their approach has outperformed previous methods in the specified benchmarks."},
{"both_well_established_benchmarks_and_a_dual_distribution_shift_benchmark": "\"Both well-established benchmarks and a dual-distribution shift benchmark\" refers to the evaluation metrics and datasets used to measure the performance of the CODA-Prompt approach. The well-established benchmarks are standard datasets commonly used in the field, while the dual-distribution shift benchmark includes semantic and covariate shifts, which are more complex and challenging scenarios."},
{"dual_distribution_shift_benchmark": "The dual-distribution shift benchmark is a testing framework designed to evaluate the performance of machine learning models under conditions of both semantic and covariate shifts. It serves as a rigorous assessment tool to measure how well a model can generalize across different types of distributional changes."},
{"dual_distribution_shift_benchmark__contains__semantic_and_covariate_shifts": "The predicate \"contains\" in this context signifies that the dual-distribution shift benchmark incorporates or includes specific types of distributional shifts\u2014namely, semantic and covariate shifts\u2014within its evaluation criteria."},
{"semantic_and_covariate_shifts": "Semantic and covariate shifts refer to two distinct types of distributional changes that can occur in data. Semantic shifts involve changes in the meaning or interpretation of features, while covariate shifts involve changes in the statistical properties of the input features. Together, they represent challenging scenarios for machine learning models."},
{"our_approach__highlights__the_potential_real_world_impact_and_generality": "The predicate \"highlights\" indicates that the approach emphasizes or brings attention to certain qualities or outcomes. In this context, it shows the significance and effectiveness of the CODA-Prompt method."},
